  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & \\le 3{x}^{2}-x+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x+x & \\le 3{x}^{2}-x+4+x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & \\le 3{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2} & \\le 4 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & \\le 4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge -4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nNow, we can use either of the two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n**Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation**\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero.\n\nSo, the inequality is true for every real number. Therefore the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\n**Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve**\n\nStarting from $-{x}^{2}\\le 4$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}-4\\le 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y=-{x}^{2}-4.$\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $-{x}^{2}-4$ is negative, the parabola opens downward (like $\u2322$). The desired inequality $(y\\le 0)$ is always satisfied. Therefore, the solution set to the given inequality includes all real numbers. That is, $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/b4b3672503fb5d0a4c7aa500793612c6cf8aed9a24101c3d659c1e80fe051511.png)",
